{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a key noble in the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and inherited the county at a young age after his father was assassinated. His early rule was marked by a regency led by his mother and later by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond III was known for his intelligence and political acumen but also faced criticism for his indecisiveness and lack of military boldness. His reign coincided with a period of increasing pressure from Muslim forces led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was married to Eschiva of Bures and later to Ioveta the Younger. He became the regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of King Baldwin III and the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. As regent Raymond struggled to maintain unity among the nobility and faced challenges from rival factions including the military orders and the Ibelin family. He was removed from the regency in 1176 due to accusations of incompetence and was replaced by the powerful Guy of Lusignan. Despite this setback Raymond remained an influential figure and continued to negotiate with Muslim leaders in an effort to preserve peace.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was one of the few Crusader nobles who attempted diplomacy with Saladin rather than constant warfare. He signed a truce with the Muslim leader in 1175 which brought temporary stability to the region. His cautious approach contrasted sharply with the aggressive policies of other nobles. In 1187 he led the defense of the Kingdom during the Battle of Hattin but was captured by Saladin along with many other Christian leaders. His capture weakened the Crusader forces and contributed to the fall of Jerusalem later that year. Raymond III died in 1187 shortly after his release from captivity. His legacy is complex as he is remembered both as a diplomat who sought peace and as a leader who failed to unite the Crusader states in the face of a growing threat. His death marked the end of an era in the County of Tripoli and the decline of its influence in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.9740936160087585}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.2927336096763611}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.5882569551467896}, {"token": " key", "logpr